# hal_server manifest — the policy for the task that enumerates the bus (#511).
#
# 🔴 ENUMERATION IS NOT DRIVING, and that is the whole reason this file says
# something no other manifest says.  A driver reads the configuration space of
# the device it has CLAIMED; a scanner reads everything, and has to, because
# the devices a scan is for are precisely the ones nobody has claimed yet.
#
# 🔑 `any' is the honest form of that sentence and the only one available: a
# manifest is written before the machine exists, so a scanner cannot name the
# devices it will find.  The kernel turns this into a claim on DEVICE_BDF_BUS
# -- the bus itself, which has no configuration space of its own and so is
# matched against no hardware class.
#
# ⚠️ On the evidence of the whole tree this is the only task that needs it.
# pci_scan.c does the scanning; every other caller of configuration space is a
# driver looking at the controller it already holds.  If a second task ever
# needs this line, that is the moment to ask whether it should be a scanner.

name hal_server

# RESOURCE_PCI_DEVICE (5), ANY device, DMA|MMIO|IRQ (0x7).
#
# ⚠️ The full 0x7 here, unlike gpu_server's MMIO-only line, and for a reason
# rather than out of symmetry: measuring a BAR means WRITING ones into it and
# reading back what sticks (#427), so the scanner writes configuration space
# as well as reading it -- twelve reads and six writes in pci_scan.c.
required 5 any 0x7
